 # Octree-based structures
 
-Also called: Sparse Voxel Octrees (SVO), Voxel Octrees, Implicit Octrees
 
 ## Overview
-Octree-based structures represent 3D volumetric data by recursively subdividing the spatial domain into eight child nodes per parent, storing data only in occupied regions to achieve compression proportional to sparsity [G]. They solve the memory and bandwidth bottlenecks of dense voxel grids by enabling efficient culling of empty space and adaptive resolution, distinguishing themselves from sibling methods like [Sparse Voxel DAGs](2791-sparse-voxel-dags.md) (which merge identical subtrees) and [Pointer Trees](4139-tree-based-structures-octree-pointer-tree-sparse-voxel-dag.md) (which use variable branching factors) by maintaining a strict binary-tree-of-cubes topology [G].
+Octree-based structures represent 3D volumetric data by recursively subdividing the spatial domain into eight child nodes per parent, storing data only in occupied regions to achieve compression proportional to sparsity [G]. They solve the memory and bandwidth bottlenecks of dense voxel grids by enabling efficient culling of empty space and adaptive resolution, distinguishing themselves from sibling methods like [Sparse Voxel DAGs](2791-sparse-voxel-dags.md) (which merge identical subtrees) and [Pointer Trees](4139-tree-based-structures-octree-pointer-tree-sparse-voxel-dag.md) (which use variable branching factors) by maintaining a strict eight-way spatial subdivision topology [G].
 
 ## Shared principles
 All structures in this family share the following mechanism:
 
-1.  **Recursive Subdivision:** The 3D domain is recursively divided into sub-volumes. In standard octrees, each node has 8 children. In pointer trees, the branching factor $N$ varies ($N^3$ children) [G].
-2.  **Sparsity Encoding:** Empty regions are represented by null pointers or absence of children, rather than storing zero-valued voxels. This provides compression proportional to the sparsity of the data [G].
-3.  **Hierarchical Traversal:** Rendering or access requires traversing from the root to the leaf. This allows skipping large empty volumes (culling) and adapting resolution to view distance or detail requirements [G].
-4.  **Spatial Locality:** Children of a node are spatially contiguous. This locality is often exploited via Morton ordering (Z-order curve) to map 3D coordinates to 1D keys, enabling efficient neighbor lookups and linear array representations in implicit variants [G].
-5.  **Subtree Merging:** The core differentiator of the DAG family is the identification of structurally identical subtrees (isomorphic nodes) and their replacement with a single shared instance. This transforms the tree topology into a graph where multiple parents may reference the same child node, eliminating redundancy .
+1. **Recursive Subdivision:** The 3D domain is recursively divided into sub-volumes [G].
+2. **Sparsity Encoding:** Empty regions are represented by null pointers or absence of children, rather than storing zero-valued voxels [G].
